"""Adult life essentials, unit 3: when life gets hard or uncertain."""
from spec import L, U

C = U('When life gets hard or uncertain', 'Morality', 'How do I act well when I am in pain, unsure, or facing the end?', [
L('Suffering that is not wasted',
  'The Church does not explain suffering away, and she does not tell people to be cheerful. She says something stranger and stronger: that Christ entered suffering, that he is present in the sick, and that pain offered in union with him can become fruitful. This lesson gives adults language for hard seasons, for visiting the sick, and for sitting with those who cannot be comforted.',
  '1500-1510,1520-1523,1521,309-314,618,164,2447',
  'Job 38:1-7;Romans 8:18-28;Colossians 1:24;Matthew 25:35-40',
  'redemption mercy hope works-of-mercy',
  'Is there a suffering in your life that you have not been able to give any meaning to?',
  'Think of one person you know who is ill, grieving, or in a hard stretch. Write what you would not say to them (the clichés), and what you could offer instead: a meal, a specific hour, a prayer you will keep. Do the first act this week.',
  'for-sick',
  id='YA-L7',
  hook='Read Job 2:11–13: the friends sit with Job for seven days and say nothing. Ask: “When was that the best thing someone did for you? When did someone say too much?”',
  teach=[
    'The Church is honest: “Illness and suffering have always been among the gravest problems confronted in human life” (CCC 1500). Illness can lead to anguish, self-absorption, even despair and revolt against God (CCC 1501). But it can also make a person more mature, helping him “discern in his life what is not essential so that he can turn toward that which is.” Both are possible; the Church names both.',
    'God is not the author of evil, though he permits it for a greater good that we cannot always see (CCC 309–314). The Catechism says it plainly: “There is not a single aspect of the Christian message that is not in part an answer to the question of evil” (CCC 309). The heart of the answer is the Cross: God did not stand outside suffering. “From the greatest moral evil ever committed, the rejection and murder of God’s only Son, caused by the sins of all men, God, by his grace that ‘abounded all the more,’ brought the greatest of goods” (CCC 312).',
    'Christ identifies with the sick: “I was sick and you visited me” (Matthew 25:36; CCC 1503–1505). He heals, and he also takes suffering into his own passion, so that those who suffer are “united to the Passion of Christ” and can offer it “for the good of the Church” (CCC 1521; Colossians 1:24). Offering is not pretending that pain is good; it is giving it, as a real gift, to the One who also bore pain.',
    'The Church’s gift for this is the Anointing of the Sick. It is not only for the dying: it is for any serious illness or the weakness of age, and it gives “a special grace of comfort, peace and courage,” a union with Christ’s Passion, and sometimes physical healing (CCC 1520–1523). And it asks something of the rest of us: to visit, to stay, to bring food, to sit in silence. The works of mercy are how most suffering is actually lightened (CCC 2447).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What has someone said to you in suffering that helped, and what hurt?',
    'Why does the Church refuse both easy answers and the answer that there is no answer?',
    'How could you be present to someone who is suffering without trying to fix it?',
    'What does it mean to “offer” suffering? What does it not mean?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What can illness do, according to CCC 1501?|Only destroy faith|Lead to anguish or despair, but also to maturity and a turn toward what is essential|Make a person holy automatically|Show that God is absent|1|CCC 1501.',
    'What does the Cross show about evil, according to CCC 312?|That evil is an illusion|That God brings the greatest good from the greatest evil|That God wills suffering for its own sake|That suffering has no meaning|1|CCC 312.',
    'Who may receive the Anointing of the Sick?|Only the dying|Anyone seriously ill or weak with age, as well as those about to have major surgery|Only priests|Only those in a hospital|1|CCC 1514–1515.',
  ],
  life='Pick one person who is suffering. Write down one thing you will do for them, specific, small, and repeatable (a Thursday call, dinner on the first of the month). Do not offer reasons. Show up, say little, and pray for them by name.',
  saint='Damien of Molokai: a Belgian priest who went to serve people with leprosy in Hawaii and caught the disease himself.',
  misc=[
    '“God sends suffering to teach me a lesson.”',
    '“Offering up” means pretending pain is good.',
    '“The Anointing of the Sick is a death sentence.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:suffering', 'apol:anointing-sick', 'room:julian-revelations'],
  mem='“We know that in everything God works for good with those who love him.” (Romans 8:28)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain, in their own words, why the Cross changes how Christians face suffering, and what they will do for one suffering person.',
  script=dict(
    open='Read Job 2:11–13 aloud. Say, “The friends came, tore their clothes, and sat with Job for seven days and nights, without a word. That was the best thing they did. Later they started to speak.” Ask, “What is hard about sitting with pain?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Start with honesty. The Catechism does not tidy suffering. Number 1500 says illness is among the gravest problems of human life. Number 1501 says it can lead to anguish, even despair. It can also make a person see what matters. It names both. You are allowed to be angry. Job was.”',
      'Say: “Where is God? The Catechism, 309, says that every part of the Christian message is, in some way, an answer to the question of evil. But the heart of the answer is not an argument. It is a person on a cross. Number 312: from the worst evil ever committed, God brought the greatest good. He did not remove suffering; he entered it.”',
      'Say: “Jesus says, ‘I was sick and you visited me.’ In the sick, we meet him. And the Church says that those who suffer can be united to his Passion and offer it for others (1521). ‘Offering’ is not pretending. It is giving a real thing to God, who can use it. Paul says in Colossians that he fills up what is lacking in Christ’s afflictions. That is a mystery; it is a gift.”',
      'Say: “The Church’s gift is the Anointing of the Sick. It is for serious illness or old age, not only for the dying. It gives courage, peace, and union with Christ. And it asks of the rest of us: visit. Bring food. Say little. Most suffering is lightened not by the right words but by the right presence.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What do you wish someone had said to you in a hard time? What do you wish they had not said?',
      'Who do you know right now who is suffering? What would a specific act of presence look like?',
      'What do you need to offer to God today, plainly and without pretending?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Give out two cards. On the first write “Do not say” and list the clichés. On the second write “Do,” and list three concrete acts.',
      'In pairs, share one name and one act. Commit to a date.',
      'Pray the prayer for the sick for that person by name.',
    ],
    close='Pray the prayer for the sick. Say, “You don’t have to fix it. You do have to stay.”'),
  tryit=['List what not to say and what to do.', 'Choose one person and one act.', 'Set the date and pray for them by name.'],
  saintStory='Jozef De Veuster was born in Belgium in 1840. He joined the Congregation of the Sacred Hearts and took the name Damien. In 1864 he was sent to Hawaii, where he was ordained a priest. At that time Hawaii was exiling people with leprosy (Hansen’s disease) to a peninsula on the island of Molokai, where they were left with little food, water or care.\nIn 1873, Damien volunteered to go. He found a place of despair and lawlessness. He built houses, dug wells, made coffins, organized a school and a choir, and cared for people’s wounds with his own hands. He ate with them and smoked their pipes, which made it clear that he was not afraid. He began to say “we lepers” when he preached.\nIn 1885 he found that he had caught the disease; he knew it when he scalded his foot in hot water and felt nothing. He kept working, and died on April 15, 1889, aged 49. Pope Benedict XVI canonized him in 2009.',
),

L('Making decisions when you do not know what to do',
  'Most important decisions are made without certainty: a job, a move, whom to marry, what to say. The Church does not promise a sign. She gives a method: form your conscience, practise prudence, ask counsel, pray, and then act, trusting that God works with imperfect choices. This lesson turns the Catechism’s teaching on conscience and prudence into a practical process an adult can use.',
  '1776-1802,1806,1787-1789,2690,2826,1731-1738',
  'James 1:5;Proverbs 3:5-6;Proverbs 15:22;Luke 14:28-32;Acts 15:28',
  'conscience prudence discernment free-will vocation',
  'What decision are you currently stuck on, and what are you waiting for to feel certain?',
  'Take one real decision. Write it as a clear question, list two or three options, then run each through the five tests: Is it good in itself? Does it fit my duties and promises? What do wise people say? Where does peace lie after prayer? What does it cost others? Choose a next step you can take this week.',
  'for-vocation',
  id='YA-L8',
  hook='Ask: “What is a decision you made quickly that turned out well? A decision you agonised over that turned out well?” Note how neither feels like what we expect. Then read Proverbs 3:5–6.',
  teach=[
    'Conscience is not a feeling. It is “a judgment of reason by which the human person recognizes the moral quality of a concrete act” (CCC 1778). It must be formed lifelong, “enlightened by faith and the Word of God, assisted by the gifts of the Holy Spirit, and guided by the advice of competent people” (CCC 1783–1785, 1798). A person must always obey the certain judgment of conscience, but is responsible for forming it (CCC 1790–1794).',
    'When the case is unclear, the Catechism gives a short, practical list: “Man is sometimes confronted by situations that make moral judgments less assured... but he must always seriously seek what is right and good.” Three rules always apply: never do evil so that good may result; the Golden Rule; charity respects the neighbor and his conscience (CCC 1787–1789). These set the boundaries; inside them there is room for judgment.',
    'Inside the boundaries, prudence leads. “Prudence is the virtue that disposes practical reason to discern our true good in every circumstance and to choose the right means of achieving it” (CCC 1806). It has three steps: take counsel, judge, and command, which means to act (Aquinas). Most people stall at the third. The Catechism also sends us to “the advice of competent people” and “the signs of the times” (CCC 1788).',
    'Prayer is not a shortcut around this. It is the climate in which the process happens: “If any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask God” (James 1:5). Ignatian discernment adds a lens: notice where peace and consolation lie, where agitation and dread, over time, not in one night. And then decide: God works through imperfect choices. A good decision does not need certainty; it needs honesty, counsel, courage, and trust (CCC 1730–1738).',
  ],
  talk=[
    'Which do you tend to skip: counsel, judgment, or action? Why?',
    'What is the difference between peace and mere comfort?',
    'Who are your “competent people,” and what are you not telling them?',
    'How do you act when you are 70 percent sure?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What is conscience, according to CCC 1778?|A feeling of guilt|A judgment of reason about the moral quality of a concrete act|The voice of the majority|A rule book|1|CCC 1778.',
    'Which rule always applies, even in doubt?|Do what feels natural|Never do evil so that good may result|Follow the crowd|Avoid all risk|1|CCC 1789.',
    'What are the steps of prudence?|Feel, decide, forget|Take counsel, judge, act|Wait, wait, wait|Pray, then ignore|1|CCC 1806 and the tradition from Aquinas.',
  ],
  life='Choose one decision you have put off. Within three days, ask two wise people (one who knows you, one who knows the field) for their honest view, write what you hear, and set a deadline to decide. Then act on the next step and notice how you feel after.',
  saint='Ignatius of Loyola: a wounded soldier who learned to read the movements of his own heart and wrote the Spiritual Exercises, a classic guide to discernment.',
  misc=[
    '“If it is God’s will, I will just know.”',
    '“Conscience means doing what I feel is right.”',
    '“A wrong decision ruins God’s plan.”',
  ],
  deeper=['room:ignatius-exercises', 'room:desales-devout-life'],
  mem='“Trust in the Lord with all your heart, and do not rely on your own insight. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make straight your paths.” (Proverbs 3:5–6)',
  assess='Ask each person to walk through the five tests on their chosen decision and state their next step and its deadline.',
  script=dict(
    open='Say, “Think of a decision that you made quickly that turned out well, and a decision you agonised over that turned out well.” Give them a minute. Ask, “What did the quick one and the long one have in common?”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “First, what conscience is not. It is not a feeling, and it is not the opinion of the crowd. The Catechism, 1778, calls it a judgment of reason about a concrete act. That means it can be educated, and it can be wrong. Our duty is to form it: Scripture, Church teaching, wise people, prayer. And then to obey it.”',
      'Say: “When we are unsure, the Catechism, 1787 to 1789, gives three boundary rules. One: never do evil that good may come of it. Two: do to others what you would have them do to you. Three: charity always respects the conscience of the neighbour. These don’t decide for you. They tell you where the fence is. Within the fence, you use prudence.”',
      'Say: “Prudence, number 1806, is the virtue that helps us see our true good in each situation and choose the right way to get there. The tradition gives three steps: take counsel, judge, and act. Most of us stall on the third. We keep taking counsel so that we do not have to decide. At some point, you decide, with what you know, and act.”',
      'Say: “And pray, not as a way to skip the work, but as the air in the room. Ask God for wisdom, as James says. Then watch over a few days where peace tends to settle and where dread does. Peace and comfort are not the same, but over time a pattern shows. And remember: God can bring good out of imperfect choices.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'Which step do you skip: counsel, judgment, or action?',
      'Who are the wise people you should be talking to, and what have you not told them?',
      'What is the fear under your delay?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out a sheet with five questions: Is it good in itself? Does it fit my duties and promises? What do wise people say? Where does peace lie after prayer? What does it cost others?',
      'Take one real decision and write the answers in silence.',
      'In pairs, state the next step and the deadline. Write both down.',
    ],
    close='Pray for vocation together. Say, “You do not have to see the whole road. You have to take the next step honestly.”'),
  tryit=['Name your decision as a question.', 'Run the five tests.', 'Write the next step and the deadline.'],
  saintStory='Íñigo López de Loyola was born in 1491 in the Basque country of Spain, the youngest of thirteen children. He grew up as a soldier and a courtier, fond of fighting, gambling and romance. In 1521, at the siege of Pamplona, a cannonball smashed one leg and wounded the other. He was carried home to recover, and he asked for novels of chivalry to pass the time. There were none in the house; he was given a life of Christ and a book on the saints.\nAs he lay reading, he noticed something. When he daydreamed about glory and romance, he felt a thrill, followed by emptiness. When he thought of imitating the saints, the joy lasted. That was the beginning of his discernment of spirits. He left for the shrine at Montserrat, spent almost a year in prayer in Manresa, and wrote the notes that became the Spiritual Exercises.\nHe studied in Paris, gathered friends including Francis Xavier, and in 1540 founded the Society of Jesus. He died in Rome in 1556.',
),

L('Death: how to live as if it is coming',
  'Everyone dies, yet we organise our lives to avoid thinking about it. The Church asks Christians to remember death, not out of morbidity but because it clarifies what matters, ends procrastination of love, and prepares us to meet Christ. This lesson pulls together the Catechism’s teaching on death, judgment, purgatory, prayer for the dead and funerals, with a practical plan for ordinary preparation.',
  '1005-1014,1020-1022,1030-1032,1681-1690,2299,1013',
  'Psalm 90:1-12;Luke 12:16-21;John 11:17-27;1 Thessalonians 4:13-18',
  'eternal-life judgment purgatory hope',
  'If you knew you had a year left, what is the first thing you would change?',
  'Do the practical work most people avoid: write a short list of what is in order (will, medical directives, who to call, funeral wishes, a letter to your family) and what is not. Choose one to complete this month. Then name one relationship you want to repair while there is time.',
  'eternal-rest',
  id='YA-L9',
  hook='Read Psalm 90:12 aloud: “So teach us to number our days that we may get a heart of wisdom.” Ask the group to write their age in years and an estimate of the number of Sundays they might have left. Say nothing. Let it sit.',
  teach=[
    'Death is not natural in the sense of the original plan: it entered through sin (CCC 1008). But Christ has “transformed” it: by his death he “destroyed death,” and for those in him, death is the gateway to a closer life with him (CCC 1009–1010). Paul writes, “For me to live is Christ, and to die is gain” (Philippians 1:21). The Catechism notes that dying in Christ is “to go to be with the Lord” (CCC 1005; 1011).',
    'But death is also serious. It ends the time in which we can choose. “Death is the end of man’s earthly pilgrimage, of the time of grace and mercy which God offers him so as to work out his earthly life in keeping with the divine plan, and to decide his ultimate destiny” (CCC 1013). That is why the Church prays “from sudden and unforeseen death, deliver us, O Lord,” which is not fear of death but desire to be ready (CCC 1014).',
    'What comes after: the particular judgment at death (CCC 1021–1022), and then heaven, purgatory, or hell. Purgatory is a final purification of those who die in God’s friendship but not yet fully holy (CCC 1030–1031). This is why the Church prays for the dead and offers Mass for them: “From the beginning the Church has honored the memory of the dead and offered prayers in suffrage for them” (CCC 1032). Grief therefore has a work to do: prayer, Masses, and works of mercy for those we love.',
    'The Church also gives us practical guidance: care for the dying with respect (CCC 2299), the Christian funeral (CCC 1680–1690), and the readiness of the faithful. Preparation is concrete: a clear will, a conversation with family, the sacraments of Penance, Eucharist and Anointing, reconciliations offered, and the daily memento mori that keeps love from being put off.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What are you avoiding by not thinking about death?',
    'How would your relationships change if you assumed each might be the last?',
    'What does it mean to pray for the dead, and how do you do it?',
    'What would you want said at your funeral, and what does that tell you about how to live now?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does CCC 1013 say about death?|It is the beginning of another earthly life|It ends the time of grace and mercy in which we decide our destiny|It is just a transition|It is something to fear without hope|1|CCC 1013.',
    'What is purgatory?|A second chance after death|A final purification for those who die in God’s friendship but are not fully holy|A place of the damned|A pagan idea|1|CCC 1030–1031.',
    'Why does the Church pray for the dead?|It is a custom|Because the dead can be helped by prayers and the Mass|Because they are lost|To earn indulgences only|1|CCC 1032.',
  ],
  life='Complete one item from your preparation list this month: a will, medical directive, a letter, or a conversation with a family member about your wishes. Then call or write one person you have been putting off.',
  saint='Stanislaus Kostka: a young Polish novice who died at 17 after a short life of intense prayer, and is invoked for a happy death.',
  misc=[
    '“Thinking about death is morbid.”',
    '“Everyone goes straight to heaven.”',
    '“Prayers for the dead are pointless.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:purgatory', 'apol:praying-for-the-dead', 'apol:heaven'],
  mem='“So teach us to number our days that we may get a heart of wisdom.” (Psalm 90:12)',
  assess='Ask each person to explain what the Church teaches about death and judgment, and name the one preparation task they will complete.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ask each person to write their age, then to subtract it from 80 and multiply by 52. Say, “That is roughly how many Sundays are left, if you are lucky.” Pause. “Now we’ll talk about it.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Start with the sting. The Catechism, 1008, says death entered through sin. We are right to find it an enemy. And Paul calls it the last enemy. But the Christian says: Christ has entered death and made it a door. Philippians 1:21: ‘For me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.’ Death is not the end of me; it is the end of my chance to choose.”',
      'Say: “Number 1013 puts it plainly: death is the end of the time of grace and mercy, in which we decide our destiny. That is why the Church asks us to pray to be delivered from sudden and unforeseen death, not out of fear, but out of the desire to be ready. The whole of the Christian life is a long preparation for that moment.”',
      'Say: “After death comes a judgment, and then heaven, purgatory or hell. Purgatory, 1030, is a final cleansing for those who die in God’s friendship but are not yet fully holy. This is why we pray for the dead, and offer the Mass for them. Grief has a job: the work of prayer. When you miss someone, pray for them. You are still in the same family.”',
      'Say: “Preparation is practical. A will. A medical directive. A letter. Telling the family what you want. Receiving the sacraments. And mending what is broken. The Church’s old practice of memento mori, remember that you will die, is not morbid. It makes love urgent. It is the end of the line for putting off the phone call.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is one thing you would do differently if you took death seriously?',
      'Who do you need to call, forgive, or thank?',
      'Who are the dead you could pray for this week?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Hand out a preparation list: will, medical directive, funeral wishes, letter to family, confession, reconciliation. Tick what is done.',
      'Choose one undone item. Write the date by which you will finish it.',
      'Write the name of the person you must contact, and what you will say.',
    ],
    close='Pray the eternal rest together for the dead you carry. Say, “Number your days, and love.”'),
  tryit=['Count your Sundays.', 'Pick one preparation task and a date.', 'Name the person you must contact.'],
  saintStory='Stanislaus Kostka was born in Poland in 1550, to a noble family. At fourteen he was sent with his older brother to Vienna to study at a Jesuit school. He was serious and devout; his brother, who thought him a prig, mocked and sometimes beat him. When Stanislaus fell ill, he was refused a priest at his lodgings. He said he saw St. Barbara in a vision, who brought him Communion, and that Mary promised he would join the Society of Jesus.\nHe recovered and set out for Rome in 1567, walking some 600 miles in disguise to escape his father. The Jesuit superior, Francis Borgia, accepted him as a novice in 1567. He lived only ten months. On August 15, 1568, the Feast of the Assumption, he died, aged 17, calmly. He had predicted it. He was canonized in 1726, and he is the patron saint of Poland and of those who seek a good death.\nThe Church does not hold him up as an example of a short life but of a ready one.',
),
])
